    "turn_text": "about, well, I've told the producers about me calling you and you came to my eighth grade class at Leesville Middle back in 1992 and spoke and all of those stories I've talked to them about. And I even told, we had Congressman David Jolly on last week, the former congressman from Florida. And your name came up. I told him how excited I was to be having you on this week and asked him if he got to work with you any in his time there. And he said, no, I didn't get to work with him as much. But he said, but my textbook, my political science textbook in college was written by him and I said, well, see, he literally wrote the book on how to be a congressman. You um you We I did an intro and I told like I said I told the story about eighth grade and I talked about how I got into politics and started being really interested in it at that point and how I consider my eighth grade year sort of the seminal moment when I thought okay, I'm gonna really pay attention to The political world and just was so interested in it and and how you were a part of that you And we talked about how you've been in Congress longer than all but just a few people, just a handful of people who are there right now. And I want to talk some about the changes that have, how much different it is today than it was when you started. But that's such a broad question that I'm not going to throw that at you. How do you feel about the level of partisanship right now that you have to deal with in Congress, whether that be January 6th type discussions and the Marjorie Taylor Green stuff for the last few weeks or just the general atmosphere of partisanship in Washington right now? What are your thoughts on it?",
