  {
    "episode_id": 83774,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83774.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, the examples you mentioned are are signs of the times. I don't know necessarily their signs of partisanship. They're certainly not signs of a kind of mainstream partisanship. I would say the party leaders are at least more responsible kind of mainstream party leaders. The Republican Party aren't crazy about Margie Taylor Greene and the kind of she brings to the house. But it is true, though, that the parties are farther apart. They're more polarized. And I think especially in the Republican Party, there's been a real tug to the ideological extreme. There's some of that in both parties, but especially among Republicans. And so it has made things much, much harder. When I first came in, I came in 1987, and the house was already becoming more organized around the two parties. We had strong party leadership. Jim Wright was a hard-charging speaker when I came in. And so it certainly wasn't all bipartisan, but there was a possibility in the committees especially of working together, working across party lines, coming to some kind of accommodation. And I really enjoyed that. I founded a productive kind of atmosphere. Then my third term got on the committee. I think most people would say is the least partisan of all appropriations. And that's still true. That's one of the reasons I like it. We work on dollars and cents matters in a fairly common sense way. So there's still some vestiges of that more cooperative kind of relationship in the house. But appropriations often get swamped by partisan forces. We end up with these end of session crises, shutdowns or near shutdowns and budget deals, and you know, all that gets very hyper. And so, yeah, it's more partisan, more divided. And I think, therefore, less enjoyable, less productive place for many members. We've had to develop strong leadership to deal with this. You know, you can't just let nature take its course in the house anymore. You've got to have centralized strong leadership. And of course, Newt Gingrich on the Republican side set the bar pretty high for that. But when Nancy Pelosi came in, she didn't go back to the old committee-centered model. She has been a strong leader as well. And I'd say she needs to be.",
