  {
    "episode_id": 83774,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83774.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "It's a good question because you're right to infer that it's not a complete transformation and it isn't. We're not a parlor. We're not, we're not, we're not regimented to that degree. And there are differentiations within appropriations, as I said, is a relatively cooperative arena, although we sometimes get swamped by these end of session showdowns. I am the chairman, I'm the founder and the chairman of a totally nonpartisan group called the House Democracy Partnership, which I really value. We've done this for 15 years now and we have just a completely congenial bipartisan approach to working with parliaments in developing countries. This was started in the house by Not a Martin Frost back when communism fell in the Eastern Europe and the house began helping these parliaments that had been just showcased parliaments under communism, helped them get up and running and become European Parliament, the Polish Parliament, the Czech, the Baltic Parliament, all of that was a, they flourished, I think, partly because they did get international support, especially in this country. Well, Gingrich let that die, and I thought that was a big mistake. So I brought it back with the help, though. It would have been impossible without the help of a Republican Speaker, Dennis Haster. And David Dryer of California, Republican and I co-led this for years. And now I'm, once again, the chairman. And we really do think it's important to send a send-off cooperation, but also to make it real in the way we work. So I must say it's one of the most enjoyable things I do.",
