  {
    "episode_id": 83774,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83774.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 168,
    "turn_text": "I'd like to see some forgiveness, but I don't think they can be completely forgiven, and I don't think the forgiveness, past a certain point, should take no regard to income levels. You know, borrowing funds under reasonable terms, and we ought to make sure the terms are reasonable. But borrowing under unreasonable terms is a legitimate way to pay for your education. It's an investment that will bear returns. And so loans are made and loans are paid back, hopefully, with some flexibility regarding people's circumstances. So I don't think you, regardless of people's circumstances or income levels, I don't think you just have a blanket forgiveness. On the other hand, There are a lot of people laboring under a lot of student debt out there. So, hey, I would think some forgiveness would be good. I think that forgiveness should be focused on lower income levels. And I also think that going forward, we need to make sure the terms of those loans are favorable and also that people can refinance the loans. Just like you can do a house loan. Why wouldn't you be able to do that?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Some forgiveness of student debt is desirable.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Complete forgiveness of student debt is not appropriate.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "A blanket forgiveness policy without considering income levels is inappropriate.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Forgiveness beyond a certain threshold should consider income levels.",
        "confidence": 0.9
